# DTF tier manifest: inline-scan-hosted-gate-2954  (#2954)
# Profile-set: filesystem / single. Entirely local (upload + policy + download),
# no upstream fetch and no internet — the default filesystem profile exercises
# it fully.
#
# WHAT IT GUARDS (#2954, Part 1 — hosted download gate):
#   `PolicyService::evaluate_artifact` enforces block_unscanned / block_on_fail /
#   max_severity, but before this fix it had exactly ONE caller — the promotion
#   gate. The ~30 per-format download handlers only consulted the quarantine
#   status via `quarantine_service::check_artifact_download`, so a hosted
#   artifact under a `block_unscanned` (or findings) policy was still downloadable
#   on the raw pull path — `block_unscanned` / `block_on_policy_violation` were a
#   false affordance. The fix folds evaluate_artifact into the shared
#   `check_artifact_download` choke point (new `enforce_download_gate`), lighting
#   up every format at once.
#
# Discriminating oracle (oracle.sh):
#   * hosted-blocked: a local maven repo with an uploaded jar AND a repo-scoped
#     scan policy `block_unscanned=true`. The jar is unscanned, so
#     GET the jar ->
#       Fixed (#2954):   403 (evaluate_artifact denies on the download path).
#       Pre-#2954:       200 (policy never consulted on download) -> FAIL.
#   * hosted-nopolicy (REGRESSION GUARD): an identical local maven repo with an
#     uploaded jar and NO scan policy. GET the jar -> 200 on BOTH images. This
#     pins the blast-radius guardrail: folding policy into the shared choke point
#     must NOT 403 downloads for repos that never opted into a scan policy
#     (evaluate_artifact no-ops to allowed when no enabled policy matches).
#
# RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED=false: the oracle drives an admin login plus a dozen
# back-to-back API + upload + download calls; the base default (ON) would
# throttle them (same rationale as the curation-* tiers).
PROFILES="storage.filesystem"
ORACLE="oracle.sh"
RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED="false"
